what The written-ASCII family named a printf argument as its one unread population and priced it from nothing. Measured over 3,682 tracked shell sources: the residue outside heredocs and comments is 11,030 characters, and 10,416 of those stand in .rish where the spoken meter already holds them. The .sh half is 614 characters that NO meter in this tree reads -- echo 360, printf 113, the rest in other positions -- so the family's own guess named the smaller half of its gap by three to one. A POSIX .sh file has no say verb, so the spoken meter never opens one, and the whole .sh emit surface stands unread. Seat a meter over what a shell script emits through echo and printf, splitting spoken from written by the line's own redirect, with the bounded quote-depth walk the family named as the reason it could not.
claim incense-section-citation-promise
seat incense
stamp 20260916.184437
epoch 1789598677
paths tools/fixtures/s/section_citation_scan.sh tools/s/section_citation_witness.rish tools/fixtures/s/section_citation_control.sh
what A living page that cites a SECTION of another page by name makes a promise no instrument reads. Links are gated two ways -- the target must open (tracked_link) and the anchor text must name the path it opens (link_text_promise) -- and both read PATHS. A section name is prose beside the path, so the card's molt of 20260912 left the compass rose sending a reader to *Now -- the live front*, *The Compass Chapter* and *Next -- the ranked remainder*, none of which stands on construction/ITINERARY.md today, with every link green. Crude census over living tracked Markdown reads 19 sites of the arrow-or-section form alone, at least four already stale. Seat a scan that reads a path token followed by an emphasised name -- after an arrow, after the word section, or directly after a link close -- and asks the cited file for a heading of that name, gating living surfaces and reading past dated testimony.
